As one of the original authors of NH++, I think this has gotten
pretty amusing. Anyway, here's the story (as best I know it):

Me (Tom Proudfoot, proudft@ocf.berkeley.edu) and Yuval (Yuval Oren,
    yuval@I have no idea) had pretty much played Nethack 3.0j to death.

It had been out for a long time, we were bored, and I started thinking
of things to add to the game. Yuval would code them and compile the thing
on his (much faster) computer. After a while, we thought the resulting
mess was pretty cool, and since Nethack 3.0j seemed to be going nowhere,
we decided to share it with other people. We had to find a name to
distinguish between the two, and settled on Nethack++ after rejecting
BloatHack and TomHack (my favorite).

So we sent it off to wuarchive and the rest was out of our hands. Someone
complained that it couldn't be called Nethack++ if it wasn't in C++, so
Yuval renamed it Nethack--, there were a few tiny bug fixes, and we
never touched it ever again. When Nethack 3.1 came out, someone else
converted Nethack-- to Nethack--3.1 and the rest is confused history.

